<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martin Mutschmann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martin Mutschmann was a German textile manufacturer and Nazi official, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiter</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> from 1925 and Reich governor of Saxony from 1933 to 1945. He controlled the state government, persecuted political opponents and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and failed to prepare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>'s air-raid defences. Captured by the Soviets in 1945, he was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-mutschmann.jpg" alt="Martin Mutschmann" width="320" height="295" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leipzig_International_Fair_1937_(Martin_Mutschmann).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mutschmann was born on 9 March 1879 in Hirschberg on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>, in the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principality-of-reuss-younger-line">Reuss-Gera</a>, and grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plauen">Plauen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Trained as an embroiderer, he worked in lace and linen factories in Plauen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herford">Herford</a> and Köln, did military service from 1901 to 1903 and in October 1907 founded his own lace factory, Mutschmann &amp; Eisentraut, in Plauen. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> he served on the Western Front with Reserve Infantry Regiment 133 until he was severely wounded in April 1916, received the Iron Cross, 2nd class, and was discharged as unfit for field service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the war he joined the nationalist and antisemitic Deutschvölkischer Schutz und Trutzbund, and in April 1922 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>, founding its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plauen">Plauen</a> branch and giving it money of his own. Though he lost his business in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>, he went on raising donations from other firms, which won him favour with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>, whom he visited in Landsberg prison. While the party was banned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch">Beer Hall Putsch</a> he led its front organisation, the National Socialist Freedom Movement, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> from August 1924, and on 27 March 1925 he became its Landesleiter, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiter</a>.</p><p>Elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> for Chemnitz-Zwickau in September 1930, he also edited the Nazi daily Der Freiheitskampf. Appointed Reichsstatthalter on 5 May 1933, he became Minister-President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> as well on 28 February 1935, displacing his rival Manfred Freiherr von Killinger, and was one of only a few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiters</a> to hold both posts. A passionate hunter, he was often accused of caring more for the hunt than for Saxony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From 1939 he was Reich Defense Commissioner, responsible for air and civil defence, and was blamed for the poor preparation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> for the bombing of 13–15 February 1945. He declared Dresden a fortress city on 14 April, ordered public mourning after Hitler's suicide and on 5 May falsely announced a great German offensive in the east. He fled as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> occupied the city on 8 May, was arrested at Tellerhäuser on 17 May and handed over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a>. Held in the Lubyanka, he was sentenced to death by the Military Collegium of the Supreme Court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> on 30 January 1947 and shot on 14 February.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Martin Mutschmann” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nazis took control in 1933, with <b>Martin Mutschmann</b> as Reich governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-saxony">Free State of Saxony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hirschberg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Lubyanka Building, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.41,11.82&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Hirschberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4820 412 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Martin Mutschmann"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 682h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 682h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.41,11.82&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
